Experiment: Reading Keyboard Input in C Using getch()
Aim:
To implement a basic keylogger in C to read keyboard input, display ASCII values of entered characters, and store the input data in text and RTF files.
Tools / Requirements:
GCC Compiler
C Programming Language
Linux Terminal
Text Editor
LibreOffice Writer
Theory:
A keylogger records keyboard input using software. In this experiment, a basic C program reads user-entered keys, displays their ASCII values, and stores the input in a file.
Types of Keyloggers:
Software Keylogger: Records keyboard input using software.
Hardware Keylogger: Records keystrokes using a physical device.
Important Functions:
getchar() – Reads a character.
putchar() – Displays a character.
fopen() – Opens or creates a file.
fputc() – Writes a character into a file.
fclose() – Closes a file.
tcgetattr() – Reads terminal settings.
tcsetattr() – Changes terminal settings.
Procedure:
Write a basic C program to read keyboard input using getchar().
Display each character along with its ASCII value.
Store the entered text in a file.
Save the output in text and RTF formats.
Compile and execute the program using GCC.
Verify the stored data in the output files.
Precautions:
Use only authorized test input.
Handle files properly.
Compile the program without errors.
Do not record anyone's keystrokes without permission.
Result:
A basic keylogger program was successfully implemented in C to read keyboard input, display ASCII values, and store the entered data in text and RTF files.
